<BlogPost slug="your-calendar-app-has-no-memory">

  <p>
    Your calendar knows a meeting happened. It doesn't know what was said in it. The decisions, the follow-ups, and the thing you promised to send by Friday end up in a notes app, a doc, or the back of a notebook, and the calendar keeps only the time and the title.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then next month you're back in the same meeting, trying to remember what you agreed last time and where you wrote it down.
  </p>

  <h2>A meeting you can write on</h2>

  <p>
    In Pikos, an event from your calendar is a page. Open it and start typing. The notes from the meeting live on the meeting, so finding them later means finding the meeting, which you already know how to do.
  </p>

  <p>
    The page works like any other in Pikos. You can tag it, set a reminder on it, and tick it off when it's done. The event's description comes in as the start of the page, and from then on it's yours to edit.
  </p>

  <h2>A repeating meeting keeps its history</h2>

  <p>
    A weekly one-on-one is one page, whichever week you open it from. Write each week's notes under the last, and what you discussed last month is a scroll up from what you're discussing today. That's the part a calendar can't do, because a calendar treats every week as a new event with nothing in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your calendar stays in charge of the time</h2>

  <p>
    The title, time, and place still come from your calendar, and Pikos shows them as they are there. Move the meeting in Google Calendar or iCloud and the page moves with it. Your notes stay put.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing you write goes back to your calendar. Your colleagues don't see your notes in the invite, and your provider never gets a copy. The notes stay on your computer, like everything else in Pikos.
  </p>

  <h2>Notes outlast the event</h2>

  <p>
    If a meeting gets cancelled after you've written on it, the page stays, with the time it had. The same goes for turning a calendar off: its events leave Pikos, but any page you wrote notes on stays. An event you never touched just goes, so a cancelled meeting doesn't leave clutter behind.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/calendar-sync">setup guide</a> connects Google, iCloud, or any CalDAV calendar in about two minutes. After that, the next meeting you open is a page waiting for notes.
  </p>

</BlogPost>
